Here is the commit message:
bugfix

Quantifiers are no Application terms any more.

Here is the root cause:
return true` branch, causing quantifiers to be misclassified as atomic. Additionally, `getArity()` and `getArg()` called `get_app_num_args()`/`get_app_arg()` unconditionally, which are only valid for `Z3_APP_AST` nodes — calling them on a quantifier AST node produces undefined or incorrect behavior. This mismatch between the actual AST structure of quantifiers and the code's assumption led to the introspection bug where `qmgr.isQuantifier()`/`isForall()` did not work correctly for Z3.

Here is the patch:

   @Override
   public boolean isAtom(Long t) {
     int astKind = get_ast_kind(z3context, t);
     switch (astKind) {
       case Z3_APP_AST:
         long decl = get_app_decl(z3context, t);
         return !NON_ATOMIC_OP_TYPES.contains(get_decl_kind(z3context, decl));
+      case Z3_QUANTIFIER_AST:
+        return false;
       default:
         return true;
     }
   }

   @Override
   public int getArity(Long t) {
+    Preconditions.checkArgument(get_ast_kind(z3context, t) == Z3_APP_AST);
     return get_app_num_args(z3context, t);
   }

   @Override
   public Long getArg(Long t, int n) {
+    Preconditions.checkArgument(get_ast_kind(z3context, t) == Z3_APP_AST);
     return get_app_arg(z3context, t, n);
   }



